Some tricks for staying alive (a little) longer:

1) Take very detailed notes as you explore (with occasional screen
captures).  Keep track of what you find (and where you find it).  Track
what the various monsters you run into carry/throw/pick up/wield/drop.
Keep track of discoveries.  Note what a shopkeeper will pay for
*nifty_unidentified_item*.  Keep track of what particular shops buy and
sell (and what various things cost).  Note what a particular shopkeeper
has in inventory (this game).  Record what you had when you died.
Write down how you died.  Keep the "You die ..." and "DYWYPI?"
screenshots with your notes.  Pay attention to *everything*, no matter
how useless it initially appears.

2) Refer to your notes often (both current game and previous games).
Eventually much of it will become instinct.  You'll have a better feel
for what various partially identified pieces of kit might be
(especially if you #name those pieces of kit as you go along).  If you
have a wand/ring/scroll/book/potion/&c you've named "costs XXX" then it
is possible to know, by price alone, what it isn't.

3) Track your prayers (turn, result, &c) and offerings (sacrifice,
message, result).  Dangerous situations become slightly less so if you
*know* it's safe to pray.

4) Slow it down, expecially when you encounter a tricky/problematic
situation.  Do whatever it takes:  Save and come back the next day.
Read a magazine.  Watch TV.  Go for a walk.  Go out and have a
<beverage of choice> with friends.  Anything to get you (and your mind)
away from the game is a good thing here.  Often, you'll think up a
solution if you step away and do something else.

5) Develop a plan and stick to it.  Know what you want to do short-term
(i.e. sacrifice until I get 'foo') and long-term (i.e. "I need to find
'foo' before I go do such-and-such" or "I want to visit 'x', 'y', and
'z' in a particular order").

6) Never be afraid to run away.  If you step downstairs and see
something you're not prepared to deal with ... run.  Go up and down
levels until you're ready to tackle it.  Visit a different dungeon
branch.  To misquote Delenn from Babylon 5 "If you value your lives, be
somewhere else."

7) Watch other players.  Peek in live, or recorded (if available).
Take notes.  You'll learn a great deal very quickly.

8) Utilize wizmode and explore mode as learning tools.  Especially if
you get in over your head.

9) Never be afraid to either ask a question or try something just for
kicks.  If you have a specifice problem, perhaps someone else knows how
to get out of it.

10) The spoilers make a very handy reference tool.  For example, I
don't have the price of every object memorized.  If I know that "foo"
costs a certain amount, the spoilers let me narrow down what it might
be.

11) Download the source code.  If you know how to program, it's a great
source for (arcane) knowledge.  Even if you don't, it can still be
educational (it's very well written and usually clear).

-cdi, whose personal preference is source first, spoilers second.



